hello everyone my name is nari Seoul and  I'm a pianist composer with a classical  music background some of you may have  seen this video that I did last year  called funk as digested by a classical  musician and if you did you're probably  well aware of the fact that funk isn't a  genre that I'm very familiar with and  honestly since then I haven't taken this  exploration that much further but  recently I revisited the genre for an  episode of sound field which is a music  channel under PBS Digital Studios  that I'm currently co-hosting with la  Buckner a fantastic drummer so a lot of  people say you should definitely check  it out so in it LA and I wrote a short  song together inspired by James Brown he  first came up with a drum track he then  helped me come up with a bass line over  the phone  and then I added five more lines on top  of it using the keyboard so given my  limitations around writing something  funky since it's not something that  comes naturally to me I had a bit of a  formula in mind to help me out and I  will emphasize the fact that yes perhaps  it's a bit of a scientific approach but  it definitely helped me out so first of  all I focused on sixteenth note  subdivisions for each beat and I looped  short punchy phrases and I made sure to  have enough gaps that were staggered  between the lines just by focusing on  having these gaps that were offset from  line to line certain lines ended up  being very syncopated now to create  these lines I found that assigning  numbers to each subdivision helped me  create syncopated lines so for all of  the beats I was thinking one two three  four one two three four one two three  four one two three four and more  specifically groups of descending  consecutive numbers between each  repetition creates a syncopated feel if  we make the stabs at four three two  it'll sound like this  and three two and three two with a blank  measure in between four three and four  three with the blank measure in between  so I didn't create all of these lines in  this fashion but it really helped me get  started and I think it's a useful  formula to just keep in mind so here's a  breakdown of the different lines I added  on top of LA's drum track  and now here's the final track so I hope  you found that interesting let me know  in the comments what you think and I'll  see you in the next video bye
 

